WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.710
This new generation of personal watercraft is
something entirely different.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.120
It does more than skim the surface.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:12.490
This watercraft dives under water and can
remain submerged at a depth of three feet or

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:13.900
more for up to a minute.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.470
It also leaps into the air and rolls with
the waves.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.500
This watercraft is painted to look like a
vintage fighter jet, and it rolls and dives

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.790
like one in the water.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.590
A worker sprays a gel coat into a mold for
the main body.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.300
This gel will become the shiny surface of
the completed fiberglass part.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:43.150
Once the gel coat has hardened, a worker
brushes a vinyl ester resin onto it.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.040
This resin is known for its water
resistance.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:52.180
He switches from a brush to a roller as he
generously applies the resin to the hardened

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:58.030
gel coat. The crew, then drapes what's
called chopped strand mat over the mold.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.250
This fabric is made of long strands of
fiberglass.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.550
The binder in it dissolves on contact with
the resin, causing the fabric to soften to

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.140
the contours of the mold.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.960
After trimming the excess material, the crew
completely saturates the strand mat with more

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.840
resin. Rolling it on firmly gets rid of air
bubbles.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.960
The top half of the main body mold has been
lined the same way.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.430
They now bolt a dorsal fin mold to the back
on the top half of the mold.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.000
The crew now joins the two halves of the
mold.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.780
As a crane lowers the top to the bottom,
workers ensure they line up perfectly and

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.910
they lock these two main molds together with
bolts.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:56.290
A worker enters through the cockpit opening
to roll on more resin.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.880
Meanwhile, another member of the crew pulls
two sheets of fiberglass fabric onto a

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:13.000
cutting machine. The top fabric is a strong,
tightly woven fiberglass, and the bottom one

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.770
is the chopped strand mat.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.860
Automated blades cut the fiberglass to
various shapes and dimensions, each one

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.800
designed for a different part of the diving
vessel Mold.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:30.050
These large rectangles will be used to mold
the sides of the watercraft.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:35.930
After folding up the pieces, the worker
hands them over to the fiberglass technician.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:43.100
The technician has just an hour to apply the
fiberglass before the recently applied resin

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.180
starts to harden.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.460
He builds up eight layers and completely
saturates each one with more resin.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:56.160
While the thicker fiberglass material
provides strength, the thinner chopped strand

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.270
mat aids adhesion and helps the fabrics
conform to the mold.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:06.870
After a three hour cure, workers have a
solid, seamless fuselage.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.160
The crane lifts the fuselage out of the mold
and moves it onto a dolly where it will stay

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.040
while the team works on the other parts of
the watercraft.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.750
The cone shaped front bumper is next.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:29.390
The worker uses only three layers of
fiberglass to make it because this bumper is

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:30.920
meant to collapse in the event of a
collision.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:37.550
For that reason, the part is also known as
the vessel's sacrificial nose.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.280
At another station, crew members lined fin
shaped molds with fiberglass and then pour

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.470
liquefied plastic into the cavity.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.540
Once cured, the plastic will anchor the top
of a steel wing shaft in the fin.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.160
A worker presses the shaft into the
liquefied plastic with the end protruding

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:06.700
from the fin. A second mold also lined with
fiberglass and filled with a plastic core,

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:07.810
goes on top.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.950
The team bolts it together and leaves the
part to cure.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:18.160
The Fins will ultimately be supported by a
triangular scaffold made of aluminum square

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.620
tubing. The worker clamps the scaffold
framework to a work table to stabilize the

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.690
framework as he welds it together.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.030
He then welds the scaffold tower to the
base.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.850
The worker drills holes for the bushings
that will be used to attach the fins to the

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:42.900
scaffold. Stay tuned for more as the team
prepares this personal diving watercraft for

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.220
its maiden voyage.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:56.100
This personal watercraft leaps, dives and
barrel rolls with the waves, but it doesn't

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:01.200
capsize. It's been engineered to return to
an upright position in the water.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:06.120
And when it comes to the fun factor, this
diving watercraft leaves other recreational

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.380
boats in its wake.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:16.660
To make the diving Watercrafts passenger
cabin, a worker sprays a gel coat onto a form

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.460
and covers it with fiberglass.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:24.400
A computer driven router then carves into
marine grade plywood to produce structural

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.530
backing for the sides of the cockpit.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:34.420
A worker slathers adhesive putty onto the
plywood part and applies more adhesive to the

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:35.830
fiberglass cockpit.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:44.160
He presses the plywood to the glued areas of
the cockpit and brushes resin onto the front

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:52.540
of the plywood. After cutting the cockpit
section in two, the crew glues the parts to

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.340
the inside of the fuselage.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:01.350
The fabricating team applies adhesive putty
to the cone shaped bumper known as the

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:02.790
sacrificial nose.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:10.450
They slide it onto the front of the fuselage
and then screw the bumper in place

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:13.000
temporarily while the adhesive sets.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:20.880
Another worker sans all the fiberglass parts
of the watercraft to remove bumps and

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.330
blemishes. This piece is the engine hatch.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:30.180
The team bolts the aluminum scaffold to the
floor of the fuselage.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:34.470
This is a test fit to confirm that the
dimensions are exactly right.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.440
As they install the scaffold, they use dummy
wing shafts to correctly position it within

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:49.200
the vessel. They confirm that the dimensions
are right and that the scaffold sits plumb to

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.790
the base of the watercraft.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:55.770
The next team inserts the hatch frame in the
rim around the cockpit.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:01.020
They'll bond the frame in place later with a
liquid plastic and adhesive putty.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:07.190
After priming the sanded surface of the
watercraft, team members lowered the 300

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.170
horsepower engine into the hatch.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.250
This is a dry fit to confirm everything
lines up correctly.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:17.300
They will eventually remove the engine for
the final paint and artwork.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:24.620
As the dry fit continues, they connect the
air tubes to the snorkel fins that deliver

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:25.790
air to the engine.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.220
The engine hatch has by now also been primed
and they fit it to the diving Watercrafts

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:44.250
main body. The crew verifies that the hatch
sits flush with the rest of the fuselage.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:50.850
A worker installs the exhaust outlet into
the hull at the rear of the watercraft.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.980
Crew members slide elevator fins into
bushings at the back.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.670
These fins will control the pitch axis of
the watercraft.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.500
Next, they install the jet propulsion
system.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.550
Back to the cockpit.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:13.590
The crew bolts the control pedals to the
floor and run cables to the jet propulsion

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.690
unit. The elevator fins and the steering
system.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:24.410
They reinstall the dive fin scaffold which
has been powder coated in a yellow colour.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.220
The crew connects the defense to the
scaffold.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:33.780
They situate the control panel just above
the control pedals.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.810
They run a metal linkage from the dive fins
to a control stick.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:48.940
They then test the control stick and confirm
it moves the dive planes easily and

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:55.260
correctly. Bucket seats made of lightweight
carbon fiber.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.600
Minimize the load for added speed.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:04.880
They took a rubber gasket into the framework
for the clear canopy that will surround the

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:11.140
occupants. They add a bead of sealant where
the gasket meets to the window frame.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:17.090
The vessel canopy is made of thick aircraft
grade acrylic.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.730
It's been tinted to repel the sun's rays and
keep the temperature cooler in the cockpit.

